  "interpretation": "In-distribution (raw): the translated Rust passes 26 of 38 assessable repair tests the original C passes; 12 tests fail on Rust where C passes. This 12 is a RAW test-script count, NOT a defect count: GNU coreutils tail `-f`/follow/pipe/wait/sysfs tests depend on inotify/timing/special filesystems and are routinely flaky, and `split` tests accumulate files across steps, so each failing case must be triaged by direct A/B of the shipped binaries before it counts as a translation defect. For THIS run that triage (docs/reviews/c2saferrust-ablation-triage.md, evidence in results/c2saferrust_triage_evidence.json) finds exactly ONE genuine in-distribution S1 -- `head -n -N` shared-fd positioning (C=50000, R=0) -- while the `split` failures do NOT reproduce in isolation (identical C/Rust file sets) and the rest are the known S2 stderr duplication or tail follow-mode environment sensitivity. So in-distribution the artifact is equivalent except for that one S1 plus the S2 stderr channel; the held-out stderr divergence (14% of error-path cases) is the measured oracle-fitting gap. Do not read the raw 12 as a non-equivalence rate."
